course: space
lesson: 13
title: Saturn's moons
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Saturn holds the record for moons: more than 270 are known, most of them small lumps, and new ones are still being found.
explain: Its giant moon Titan is bigger than Mercury and wrapped in a thick orange haze, with rivers, lakes and seas, but they are full of liquid methane and ethane, not water.
explain: Imagine a rainy day at minus 180 degrees: on Titan the rain is natural gas falling as liquid, filling lakes you could sail a boat on.
explain: Little Enceladus is like a garden sprinkler made of ice: jets of water burst from cracks at its south pole, from an ocean hidden underneath, and the spray feeds one of Saturn's rings.
explain: Other moons are odd too: Mimas looks like the Death Star, Iapetus is half black and half white, Hyperion is a tumbling sponge, and tiny shepherd moons herd the ice of the rings.
fact: Saturn has the most known moons of any planet, more than 270, and the count is still rising.
fact: Titan is Saturn's biggest moon, larger than the planet Mercury.
fact: Titan is the only moon with a thick atmosphere.
fact: Titan has rivers, lakes and seas, but they are filled with liquid methane and ethane, not water.
fact: In 2005 the European Huygens probe parachuted onto Titan, the most distant landing ever made.
fact: NASA's Dragonfly is a nuclear-powered drone helicopter due to launch in 2028 and fly between sites on Titan in the 2030s.
fact: Saturn's small moon Enceladus sprays jets of water ice from cracks near its south pole, from a hidden ocean below.
fact: The spray from Enceladus feeds Saturn's wide, faint E ring.
fact: The moon Mimas has a huge crater called Herschel that makes it look like the Death Star from Star Wars.
fact: Iapetus has one side as dark as coal and the other as bright as snow, and a strange ridge round its equator.
fact: Hyperion is a lumpy moon like a sponge, full of deep craters, and it tumbles chaotically, in a messy, unpredictable way, as it orbits.
fact: Small moons such as Prometheus and Pandora shepherd the narrow F ring, keeping its ice in a thin, sharp-edged band as sheepdogs keep sheep together.
fact: The tiny moons Pan and Daphnis orbit inside gaps in Saturn's main rings.
exercise: Which planet has the most known moons? | Saturn, with more than 270 | Jupiter, with exactly 4 | The Earth, with 1 | Neptune, with more than 1,000
exercise: Which is Saturn's biggest moon? | Titan | Enceladus | Mimas | Ganymede
exercise: Titan is larger than which planet? | Mercury | The Earth | Mars | Jupiter
exercise: What makes Titan different from every other moon? | It has a thick atmosphere | It has no craters at all | It is made only of iron | It shines with its own light
exercise: What fills Titan's rivers, lakes and seas? | Liquid methane and ethane | Salty liquid water like our seas | Melted rock from its volcanoes | Liquid iron from deep inside it
exercise: What did the Huygens probe do in 2005? | Parachuted onto Titan, the most distant landing ever | Crashed into Saturn's clouds on purpose to measure them | Landed on Mars with a parachute | Flew past Pluto and took photos
exercise: What is Dragonfly? | A nuclear-powered drone helicopter for Titan | A space telescope that will look at Saturn's rings | A boat to sail Titan's seas | A rocket that will land on Enceladus
exercise: When is Dragonfly due to launch? | In 2028 | In 2005 | In 1990 | In 2100
exercise: What does Enceladus spray from cracks near its south pole? | Jets of water ice from a hidden ocean | Hot lava from deep volcanoes under its crust | Clouds of black dust | Liquid methane from its lakes
exercise: What does the spray from Enceladus feed? | Saturn's wide, faint E ring | Titan's methane lakes and seas | Saturn's hexagon-shaped storm | The Cassini Division in the rings
exercise: Why does Mimas look like the Death Star from Star Wars? | It has a huge crater called Herschel | It has a giant volcano on top | It is shaped like a cube with sharp corners | It has a ring round it
exercise: What is strange about Iapetus? | One side is dark as coal, the other bright as snow | It is perfectly round and smooth | It glows red at night | It has a thick atmosphere like Titan
exercise: What else is odd about Iapetus? | A strange ridge round its equator | A deep ocean lying on top of its ice | Rings of its own like Saturn | A tail like a comet
exercise: How does Hyperion move as it orbits? | It tumbles chaotically | It always shows the same face | It does not spin at all | It spins backwards very smoothly
exercise: What does Hyperion look like? | A lumpy sponge full of deep craters | A smooth white ball with no craters at all | A flat disc like a coin | A ball covered in lakes
exercise: What do small moons like Prometheus and Pandora do? | Shepherd the narrow F ring | Spray water into the E ring | Stir up the hexagon storm on Saturn | Pull Saturn towards Jupiter
exercise: Where do the tiny moons Pan and Daphnis orbit? | Inside gaps in the main rings | Far outside all of Saturn's rings | On the surface of Titan | Round Enceladus
test: Leo says Titan's lakes would be great for a swim, it's just water innit. right? | No, they are liquid methane and ethane at about minus 180 degrees | Yes, they are warm salty water, heated by Saturn's glow all year round | Yes, but only in the summer | No, they are boiling hot lava
test: Why would scientists be excited to fly through the spray of Enceladus? | It comes from a hidden ocean they could sample | It is made of pure gold dust | It would clean the spacecraft | It shows where Saturn's hexagon is
test: In a film, a spaceship lands on Titan and sees a clear blue sky with no clouds. What is wrong? | Titan has a thick hazy atmosphere | Titan has no sky because it has no air | Titan's sky is always black with stars | Nothing, that is exactly right
test: If Saturn's tiny shepherd moons vanished, what would most likely happen to the narrow F ring? | It would spread out and lose its sharp edges | It would become a new moon | It would fall into Titan | It would turn into liquid
test: this kid in my class says Jupiter's got the most moons. ugh, is he right or not | No, Saturn has the most known moons | Yes, Jupiter has the most because it is the biggest | No, the Earth has the most | No, Mars has the most
test: Which moon would make the best photo for a Star Wars fan? | Mimas, with its huge crater Herschel | Titan, with its thick haze | Iapetus, with its ridge | Enceladus, with its jets
test: What makes a landing on Titan so special? | It is the most distant landing ever made | It was the first landing on any moon | It was made by astronauts who walked about | It was made on a water ocean
test: Why does Dragonfly need to fly instead of drive? | To hop between sites far apart on Titan | Because Titan has no solid ground at all | Because wheels melt on Titan | Because flying is not possible on Earth
test: You look at Iapetus from two sides on two days. What do you see? | One side dark and one side bright | Both sides exactly the same dull grey colour | Both sides hidden under a thick orange haze | One side with a big volcano erupting lava
test: Which is true? | Hyperion tumbles chaotically and looks like a sponge | Hyperion is Saturn's biggest moon | Hyperion has a thick atmosphere | Hyperion sprays jets of water
test: wait so how many moons has Saturn got, like 20? | More than 270, and more keep being found | Exactly 20, and that number never changes now | Just Titan | About 2
exam: Identify the satellite of Saturn which possesses a substantial atmosphere. | Titan | Mimas | Hyperion | Pandora
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the liquids present on the surface of Titan. | They consist of methane and ethane | They consist of salt water similar to terrestrial oceans | They consist of molten rock | They consist of liquid iron
exam: Ascertain the origin of the material which supplies Saturn's E ring. | The jets of water ice emitted by Enceladus | Volcanic ash erupted from the surface of Titan | Dust from the Cassini spacecraft | Fragments of the moon Mimas
exam: State the distinction attributed to the descent of the Huygens probe in the year two thousand and five. | It constitutes the most distant landing ever accomplished | It constitutes the first landing upon the Moon | It was accomplished by a human crew | It was the first landing upon Mars
